{\info{\title Hi, this is Quill}{\author Liminal HQ Labs}}
\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440
\pard\sb240\sa120\f0\fs32\b @ICON64@\~Hi, this is Quill\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120\fs24 You are typing into a word processor written in Rust. One core, compiled for MS-DOS, Windows 3.1, Windows 95/98/Me and Linux. @WHICH@\par
\pard\sa120\tx1400\tx3000\tx4900\tx6900 \b Runs on:\b0\tab MS-DOS\tab Windows 3.x\tab Windows 9x\tab Linux\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\fs32\b Try it\b0\fs24\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'95}}\li360\fi-360\sa60 Press \b Ctrl+B\b0  for bold, change the \i font\i0 , drag the tab stops on the ruler.\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'95}}\li360\fi-360\sa60 \b Edit \'9b Spelling\b0  checks Canadian, US and UK English.\par
\pard{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'95}}\li360\fi-360\sa120 \b File \'9b Save\b0  writes a .QLD file that opens the same on every one of those systems.\par
\pard\sb240\sa120\fs32\b How did they do that?\b0\fs24\par
\pard\sa120 A small \i no_std\i0  Rust core does the layout, formatting and file formats. Thin shells draw it with each system\rquote s own GDI, VGA or terminal. No runtime, no C library.\par
\pard\sb120\sa0 @ICON24@\~\i Quill is an experiment from Liminal HQ Labs.\i0\par
}
